// A SHADOWED NAME must not make the B87 dropck rule fire about a DIFFERENT
// binding that happens to share the word.
//
// `g` borrows the OUTER `outer`, which outlives it. The inner block declares
// its own `outer`, an `i32` nobody borrows, and that block's exit used to be
// reported as `g`'s source dying — because `pop_scope` compared the recorded
// source to the dying frame BY NAME. Measured 2026-08-25 with a pair differing
// in ONE property, the inner local's spelling: named `outer` the program was
// refused, named `zz` it compiled.
//
// `RefSrc` and `dying_binding` already existed — §B6 was converted the round
// before, and this was the sibling twenty lines above it, written but not read.
// The slot is resolved where the name is collected, which is the only point at
// which it is knowable.
//
// The refuse twin is `fail/bc_dropck_source_dies_first_fail`: this pair is what
// separates "keys on identity" from "stopped checking".
package bc_dropck_shadowed_name_admit;
use logos.lang.drop;
extern fn printf(fmt: *const u8, ...) -> i32;
fn pr(fmt: str, v: i64) { unsafe { printf(fmt.as_ptr(), v); } }

// ⚠ THE STDLIB LANG ITEM, NOT A LOCAL `trait Drop`. The rule this pair is
// about — B87 dropck — is ABOUT the `Drop` lang item, and a locally declared
// `trait Drop` is an ordinary trait that implements nothing (rustc: `Drop` is
// selected by `#[lang = "drop"]`, never by spelling). While borrow_check's
// drop set was keyed on the SYMBOL SPELLING `__drop` the distinction was
// invisible; it no longer is, so the pair states its own subject.
struct H<'a> { r: &'a i64 }
impl<'a> Drop for H<'a> { fn drop(self: &mut H<'a>) { } }

fn main() -> i32 {
    let outer: i64 = 7i64;
    let g: H;
    g = H { r: &outer };
    { let outer: i32 = 2i32; let _u: i32 = outer; }
    pr("%ld\n", *g.r);
    return 0i32;
}
